{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nh77ompXfZvirwfQbDxYfx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Discussion}\n2\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n3\t\n4\t\\paragraph{Implications for deployment.}\n5\tA uniform compression budget implicitly accepts order-of-magnitude severity penalties during low-sparsity reasoning phases. Pilot evaluation ($n{=}30$, $s{=}0.25$; Appendix~\\ref{app:pilot-mitigation}) achieved 18\\% oracle recovery (95\\% CI: $[-39\\%, 45.8\\%]$), falling below the pre-registered 30\\% gate; the CI spans zero, indicating no statistically distinguishable improvement. Effective phase-aware allocation likely requires finer phase boundary estimation than current proxies provide. Additional mechanisms (e.g., strategy switching, adaptive thresholding) warrant investigation. We hypothesize that phase-modulated severity may extend to alternative attention-mass-based scoring methods (H$_2$O \\cite{h2o-neurips2023}, SnapKV \\cite{snapkv-neurips2024}, PyramidKV \\cite{pyramidkv-colm2025}), which remains an open question for future work. Extended discussion in Appendix~\\ref{app:extended-discussion}.\n6\t\n7\t\\paragraph{Reasoning depth scaling and architecture dependence.}\n8\tStrategy specificity is not a fixed property but varies along at least two axes. First, it scales with reasoning chain depth: MATH-500 ($15$--$17{\\times}$) $>$ GSM8K (${\\sim}2{\\times}$ point-estimate ratio; random non-significant at $n{=}50$), with within-MATH-500 correlational support (Spearman $\\rho > 0$, $p < 0.02$ between trace length and specificity). Second, it is architecture-dependent: LlamaForCausalLM exhibits significant random phase gradients ($d_z{=}0.41$--$0.46$), reducing the importance/random ratio to $2.0$--$2.6{\\times}$ compared with $15$--$17{\\times}$ on DeepSeek (Qwen2-based). This suggests that the degree to which random eviction is phase-insensitive depends on architectural details of the attention implementation. The depth-scaling pattern is consistent with the discrimination collapse mechanism: longer low-sparsity phases provide more tokens over which pruning-degraded importance scores accumulate errors, amplifying the gap between importance-based and random eviction. The practical implication holds regardless of architecture: as models generate longer reasoning chains (a trend driven by test-time compute scaling~\\cite{snell-2024-scaling-test-time}), the penalty for naive compound compression grows superlinearly, though the relative advantage of strategy-aware allocation varies by model family.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Diagnostic framework value.}\n11\tWhile we do not propose a new compression method, the phase-gated analysis framework has direct engineering utility: (1)~the Interaction Index can serve as a per-phase evaluation metric for assessing the phase robustness of any new compound compression scheme; (2)~the dose-response curves (Figure~\\ref{fig:hero}) provide target operating points for phase-aware controllers, e.g., maintaining $s \\leq 0.20$ during low-sparsity phases to keep $\\Delta < 1.5$; (3)~the strategy specificity finding carries a concrete engineering implication: importance-based eviction under compound sparsity requires phase-aware protection that random eviction does not, because the interaction is structurally coupled to the scoring mechanism rather than to compression magnitude alone.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Recency control as methodological contribution.}\n14\tThe use of recency-based eviction as a scoring-independent control (\\S\\ref{sec:scoring-control}) provides a principled falsification test that, to our knowledge, has no precedent in the KV cache compression literature. Prior work employs recency eviction (StreamingLLM~\\cite{streamingllm-iclr2024}) or H$_2$O scoring~\\cite{h2o-neurips2023} as performance baselines, but not as controls for isolating the contribution of the scoring mechanism itself. The null result ($d_z{=}0.083$, NS at $s{=}0.15$, $n{=}100$) confirms that the phase gradient is attention-score-specific rather than an artifact of phase segmentation or positional structure, strengthening the causal interpretation of strategy specificity as a property of score-dependent token selection.\n15\t\n16\t\\paragraph{Evidential status: mechanism discrimination, not causal proof.}\n17\tThe triangulation across importance, random, and recency eviction constitutes \\emph{mechanism discrimination}: we identify attention-score-based selection as the specific mediating pathway for phase-dependent severity, by showing the gradient's presence under score-based eviction and absence under score-independent controls. This is discriminative evidence, not intervention-based causal proof. Full causal demonstration would require directly manipulating score quality (e.g., injecting calibrated noise into attention scores before eviction) and observing a corresponding change in gradient magnitude, which we leave to future work. Our contribution is identifying the mediating pathway with sufficient specificity to guide system design: practitioners can use the finding that score-independent eviction eliminates the phase gradient to inform compression strategy selection, regardless of whether the underlying mechanism is fully causally resolved.\n18\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UnNfNKkkjeQrNwTuPxrUhk","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"e08b0b6f-5eeb-40d7-9b11-542eb83e6656","timestamp":"2026-05-17T01:58:47.678Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AQjWMk7HyWvKiAYiKr4Yqh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity: Strategy-Specific Severity Emerges from the Interaction of Head Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (joint head pruning + KV cache eviction) produces phase-dependent interaction severity that varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper, not a method contribution.\n\n**Five stated contributions:**\n1. Discovery of phase-dependent compound sparsity severity as emergent property, replicated across 3 benchmarks and 3 architectures\n2. Quantified dose-response relationship with sub-exponential growth\n3. Three-way dissociation (importance sig, random weak/null, recency null) as novel experimental design for ruling out confounds\n4. Discrimination collapse as primary mechanism (Gini/CV decrease under head pruning)\n5. Cross-benchmark analysis: strategy specificity correlates with reasoning chain structure, not task difficulty\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Compound Compression: SparK [1] (pruning+quantization, uniform super-additive), SLiM [2] (joint pruning-eviction, no phase analysis)\n- Phase-Aware KV Cache: ThinKV [3] (phase-aware single-axis), R-KV [4] (reasoning-optimized, single-axis)\n- Compression Phase Transitions: [5] (physics of KV cache compression, orthogonal \"phase\" meaning)\n- Importance Score Collapse: HIES [6] (entropy-based compensation, single-axis)\n- Adaptive Eviction: Lethe [7], H2O [8], StreamingLLM [9]\n\nTotal references: 9\n\n**Method details:**\n- Interaction Index: II_i = PPL(both) - PPL(prune) - PPL(evict) + PPL(base)\n- Phase segmentation via tercile splits of uncompressed per-token PPL\n- Three eviction strategies: importance (H2O), random, recency\n- Paired Wilcoxon with Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs\n- Models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (all 7-8B)\n- Benchmarks: MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, GSM8K\n\n**Key results:**\n- Importance gradient on GPQA: 0.947 (s=0.15) to 10.68 (s=0.30), dz=0.72–1.22\n- Three-way dissociation at s=0.25: importance 8.62 vs random 0.76 vs recency ≈0\n- Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH-500 (15–17×) > GPQA (7.8–11.3×) > GSM8K (2.3–3.8×)\n- LLaMA ratio only 2.0–2.6× (architecture-dependent)\n- Pilot phase-aware allocation: 18% PPL recovery but CIs cross zero\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** 7B models only, GSM8K ratios modest, recency n=100, no wall-clock throughput, empirical understanding not deployable method.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions with specific, evidence-based assessments:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** (weight: highest)\n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely new?\n   - Is the three-way dissociation experimental design novel?\n   - Is discrimination collapse a new mechanism or repackaging of known phenomena?\n   - How does this advance the field beyond existing work on compound compression?\n   - Is this an empirical finding paper with sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage** (weight: high)\n   - 9 references total — is this sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there major missing related works? Consider:\n     * KV cache compression methods beyond H2O/StreamingLLM\n     * Structured pruning literature\n     * Compound/joint compression beyond SparK/SLiM\n     * Phase/stage analysis in reasoning\n     * Attention score analysis literature\n   - Is the positioning against prior work accurate?\n   - Does the paper honestly characterize its incremental contribution vs prior work?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact** (weight: high)\n   - Does this finding change how practitioners think about compound compression?\n   - Is the lack of a method contribution a problem?\n   - Would this be cited and built upon?\n\n4. **Presentation Quality** (weight: medium)\n   - Is the paper well-written and clearly structured?\n   - Are claims well-supported by evidence?\n   - Any overclaiming?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — list each weakness with severity (minor/moderate/major/critical)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — specific questions that would affect your score\n\n7. **Score** — provide a single score (1-6) with brief justification\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":23668,"cache_read_input_tokens":24213,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":23668},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"03fa18a0-3606-4e46-9b95-3ffc8114db3d"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aceb691885f65aaf3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity: Strategy-Specific Severity Emerges from the Interaction of Head Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (joint head pruning + KV cache eviction) produces phase-dependent interaction severity that varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper, not a method contribution.\n\n**Five stated contributions:**\n1. Discovery of phase-dependent compound sparsity severity as emergent property, replicated across 3 benchmarks and 3 architectures\n2. Quantified dose-response relationship with sub-exponential growth\n3. Three-way dissociation (importance sig, random weak/null, recency null) as novel experimental design for ruling out confounds\n4. Discrimination collapse as primary mechanism (Gini/CV decrease under head pruning)\n5. Cross-benchmark analysis: strategy specificity correlates with reasoning chain structure, not task difficulty\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Compound Compression: SparK [1] (pruning+quantization, uniform super-additive), SLiM [2] (joint pruning-eviction, no phase analysis)\n- Phase-Aware KV Cache: ThinKV [3] (phase-aware single-axis), R-KV [4] (reasoning-optimized, single-axis)\n- Compression Phase Transitions: [5] (physics of KV cache compression, orthogonal \"phase\" meaning)\n- Importance Score Collapse: HIES [6] (entropy-based compensation, single-axis)\n- Adaptive Eviction: Lethe [7], H2O [8], StreamingLLM [9]\n\nTotal references: 9\n\n**Method details:**\n- Interaction Index: II_i = PPL(both) - PPL(prune) - PPL(evict) + PPL(base)\n- Phase segmentation via tercile splits of uncompressed per-token PPL\n- Three eviction strategies: importance (H2O), random, recency\n- Paired Wilcoxon with Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs\n- Models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (all 7-8B)\n- Benchmarks: MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, GSM8K\n\n**Key results:**\n- Importance gradient on GPQA: 0.947 (s=0.15) to 10.68 (s=0.30), dz=0.72–1.22\n- Three-way dissociation at s=0.25: importance 8.62 vs random 0.76 vs recency ≈0\n- Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH-500 (15–17×) > GPQA (7.8–11.3×) > GSM8K (2.3–3.8×)\n- LLaMA ratio only 2.0–2.6× (architecture-dependent)\n- Pilot phase-aware allocation: 18% PPL recovery but CIs cross zero\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** 7B models only, GSM8K ratios modest, recency n=100, no wall-clock throughput, empirical understanding not deployable method.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions with specific, evidence-based assessments:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** (weight: highest)\n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely new?\n   - Is the three-way dissociation experimental design novel?\n   - Is discrimination collapse a new mechanism or repackaging of known phenomena?\n   - How does this advance the field beyond existing work on compound compression?\n   - Is this an empirical finding paper with sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage** (weight: high)\n   - 9 references total — is this sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there major missing related works? Consider:\n     * KV cache compression methods beyond H2O/StreamingLLM\n     * Structured pruning literature\n     * Compound/joint compression beyond SparK/SLiM\n     * Phase/stage analysis in reasoning\n     * Attention score analysis literature\n   - Is the positioning against prior work accurate?\n   - Does the paper honestly characterize its incremental contribution vs prior work?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact** (weight: high)\n   - Does this finding change how practitioners think about compound compression?\n   - Is the lack of a method contribution a problem?\n   - Would this be cited and built upon?\n\n4. **Presentation Quality** (weight: medium)\n   - Is the paper well-written and clearly structured?\n   - Are claims well-supported by evidence?\n   - Any overclaiming?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — list each weakness with severity (minor/moderate/major/critical)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — specific questions that would affect your score\n\n7. **Score** — provide a single score (1-6) with brief justification\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","uuid":"9b98d11c-dc10-4793-81dd-21d4e20e6ff5","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity: Strategy-Specific Severity Emerges from the Interaction of Head Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (joint head pruning + KV cache eviction) produces phase-dependent interaction severity that varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper, not a method contribution.\n\n**Five stated contributions:**\n1. Discovery of phase-dependent compound sparsity severity as emergent property, replicated across 3 benchmarks and 3 architectures\n2. Quantified dose-response relationship with sub-exponential growth\n3. Three-way dissociation (importance sig, random weak/null, recency null) as novel experimental design for ruling out confounds\n4. Discrimination collapse as primary mechanism (Gini/CV decrease under head pruning)\n5. Cross-benchmark analysis: strategy specificity correlates with reasoning chain structure, not task difficulty\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Compound Compression: SparK [1] (pruning+quantization, uniform super-additive), SLiM [2] (joint pruning-eviction, no phase analysis)\n- Phase-Aware KV Cache: ThinKV [3] (phase-aware single-axis), R-KV [4] (reasoning-optimized, single-axis)\n- Compression Phase Transitions: [5] (physics of KV cache compression, orthogonal \"phase\" meaning)\n- Importance Score Collapse: HIES [6] (entropy-based compensation, single-axis)\n- Adaptive Eviction: Lethe [7], H2O [8], StreamingLLM [9]\n\nTotal references: 9\n\n**Method details:**\n- Interaction Index: II_i = PPL(both) - PPL(prune) - PPL(evict) + PPL(base)\n- Phase segmentation via tercile splits of uncompressed per-token PPL\n- Three eviction strategies: importance (H2O), random, recency\n- Paired Wilcoxon with Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs\n- Models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (all 7-8B)\n- Benchmarks: MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, GSM8K\n\n**Key results:**\n- Importance gradient on GPQA: 0.947 (s=0.15) to 10.68 (s=0.30), dz=0.72–1.22\n- Three-way dissociation at s=0.25: importance 8.62 vs random 0.76 vs recency ≈0\n- Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH-500 (15–17×) > GPQA (7.8–11.3×) > GSM8K (2.3–3.8×)\n- LLaMA ratio only 2.0–2.6× (architecture-dependent)\n- Pilot phase-aware allocation: 18% PPL recovery but CIs cross zero\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** 7B models only, GSM8K ratios modest, recency n=100, no wall-clock throughput, empirical understanding not deployable method.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions with specific, evidence-based assessments:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** (weight: highest)\n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely new?\n   - Is the three-way dissociation experimental design novel?\n   - Is discrimination collapse a new mechanism or repackaging of known phenomena?\n   - How does this advance the field beyond existing work on compound compression?\n   - Is this an empirical finding paper with sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage** (weight: high)\n   - 9 references total — is this sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there major missing related works? Consider:\n     * KV cache compression methods beyond H2O/StreamingLLM\n     * Structured pruning literature\n     * Compound/joint compression beyond SparK/SLiM\n     * Phase/stage analysis in reasoning\n     * Attention score analysis literature\n   - Is the positioning against prior work accurate?\n   - Does the paper honestly characterize its incremental contribution vs prior work?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact** (weight: high)\n   - Does this finding change how practitioners think about compound compression?\n   - Is the lack of a method contribution a problem?\n   - Would this be cited and built upon?\n\n4. **Presentation Quality** (weight: medium)\n   - Is the paper well-written and clearly structured?\n   - Are claims well-supported by evidence?\n   - Any overclaiming?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — list each weakness with severity (minor/moderate/major/critical)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — specific questions that would affect your score\n\n7. **Score** — provide a single score (1-6) with brief justification\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"693e238e-989d-4215-8cce-77e24025dba3","timestamp":"2026-05-17T01:58:51.195Z"}
