{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017QuaJK3MqS69vEJnp32hqt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017EzyaiZNMX7ZpbwDY7k4mx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_11/projects/phase-gated-compound-sparse/artifacts/_project/paper/background.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4169,"cache_read_input_tokens":49281,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4169,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":68,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"c587c133-4963-4f23-b9e9-55b976a98731"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017EzyaiZNMX7ZpbwDY7k4mx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% Section 3: Background and Problem Setup\n2\t% Note: eq:ii and eq:gradient are the canonical definitions.\n3\t% Full formulation details in Appendix (app:formulation-details).\n4\t\n5\t\\section{Background and Problem Setup}\n6\t\\label{sec:background}\n7\t\n8\tWe formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\\ref{app:formulation-details}.\n9\t\n10\t\\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}\n11\tLet $s \\in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \\emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \\cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \\emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \\emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \\emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \\cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \\emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \\cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \\cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.\n12\t\n13\t\\paragraph{Interaction Index.}\n14\tFor a text segment, let $\\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \\in \\{d, a, k, b\\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \\text{PPL}_c / \\text{PPL}_d$, and:\n15\t\\begin{equation}\n16\t    \\mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.\n17\t    \\label{eq:ii}\n18\t\\end{equation}\n19\t$\\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null as primary: it assumes $r_b = r_a + r_k - 1$ (compound loss equals sum of individual losses), the most conservative independence assumption. Although the two axes are mechanistically linked (\\S\\ref{sec:analysis}), rejection ($\\mathrm{II} > 0$) establishes super-additivity regardless of null specification. Appendix~\\ref{app:multiplicative-null} reports parallel results under the multiplicative null ($r_b = r_a \\cdot r_k$); the phase-severity gradient is preserved ($G_{\\times}{=}21$--$64{\\times}$), supporting null-choice invariance. The II distribution is heavily right-skewed; we report medians throughout.\n20\t\n21\t\\paragraph{Severity gradient.}\n22\tTo capture phase variation, the per-problem \\emph{amplification ratio} is:\n23\t\\begin{equation}\n24\t    G^{(i)} = \\frac{\\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\,\\textit{low}}}{\\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\,\\textit{high}}},\n25\t    \\label{eq:gradient}\n26\t\\end{equation}\n27\twhere $\\mathrm{II}^{(i)}_{\\,\\textit{low/high}}$ is the interaction index in the low/high-sparsity phase for problem $i$. We report $G = \\widetilde{G^{(i)}}$, the median across problems, with bootstrap CIs. We use the median of per-problem ratios (not the ratio of medians) to preserve per-problem pairing; see Appendix~\\ref{app:profiling-subset} for comparison. $G$ serves as an auxiliary descriptive ratio that illustrates the degree of phase concentration; the primary statistical evidence for the severity gradient rests on $\\Delta$ and paired Wilcoxon tests (\\S\\ref{sec:phase-amplification}).\n28\t\n29\t\\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}\n30\t\\label{par:phase-segmentation}\n31\tInspired by ThinKV's phase-aware perspective on reasoning-time compression~\\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}, each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, using a quantile-based segmentation rather than explicit reasoning delimiters (e.g., \\texttt{<think>} tokens). Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). We note that this segmentation is an \\emph{operationalization choice}: attention sparsity serves as a continuous proxy for reasoning phase rather than a theoretically grounded boundary. We validate robustness to segmentation granularity (tercile/quartile/quintile; Appendix~\\ref{app:seg-robust}) and minimum phase length (Appendix~\\ref{app:mpl-sensitivity}). Validation against DeepSeek's explicit \\texttt{<think>} markers using adaptive per-sequence quantile segmentation yields substantial agreement ($\\kappa{=}0.88$, AUC-ROC${=}0.98$; $n{=}48$; Appendix~\\ref{app:think-validation}). Importantly, phases reflect genuine computational mode differences even under the dense (uncompressed) baseline: per-phase dense perplexity increases monotonically from high ($1.10$) through mid ($1.17$) to low ($1.18$), confirming that low-sparsity phases correspond to inherently harder computation; the phase gradient in compound severity (\\S\\ref{sec:results}) thus amplifies a pre-existing computational asymmetry rather than creating one. Details in Appendix~\\ref{app:formulation-details}.\n32\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"26062013-38a8-408e-8bb5-c766b2e91f62","timestamp":"2026-05-17T01:59:34.552Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"ae7e8093c58aaec24","tool_use_id":"toolu_01UnNfNKkkjeQrNwTuPxrUhk","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":58413,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":136666},"uuid":"8484dd9b-ca8b-4e7a-9744-3c99595c85d2","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UnNfNKkkjeQrNwTuPxrUhk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough reading of the paper, I now have enough information to write a comprehensive review focused on methods and experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity (R1 — Methods & Experiments)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**Interaction Index (II):**\nThe II definition (Eq. 1: II = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1) is well-motivated and standard for measuring super-additivity. The choice to normalize by dense-baseline perplexity before computing the interaction is appropriate. The verification under the multiplicative null (Appendix A) strengthens confidence that the findings are not null-choice-dependent. One concern: II is computed on per-phase *segments* of varying length, meaning II magnitudes are not directly comparable across phases unless segment-level perplexity is properly averaged. The paper uses medians of per-problem paired differences, which appropriately handles this through pairing.\n\n**Phase Segmentation:**\nThe tercile-split approach based on per-position attention sparsity from the *uncompressed* model is a reasonable operationalization. The validation against `<think>` tokens (kappa=0.88, AUC=0.98) on n=48 GSM8K problems is encouraging but narrow. Robustness to quartile/quintile splits and min_phase_length variation is well-documented.\n\n**Potential circularity concern:** The paper acknowledges that importance-based eviction uses attention scores, and phase segmentation also uses attention sparsity. However, phases are defined from the *dense* model, not the compressed model, which largely eliminates circularity. The recency control further addresses this. I find the circularity concern adequately mitigated.\n\n**Statistical framework:** Wilcoxon signed-rank (paired, nonparametric) is appropriate given the heavily right-skewed distributions (mean/median ratios up to 43:1). Bonferroni correction with k=4 per family is conservative and clearly justified. The Jonckheere-Terpstra test for dose-response monotonicity is the correct choice. Bootstrap CIs (B=10,000) are standard.\n\n**Score: Sound.** The methodology is well-specified and the choices are defensible.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Three eviction strategies (importance, random, recency) with recency as a scoring-independent control is an excellent experimental design choice. This is the paper's strongest methodological contribution.\n- Dose-response across 4 sparsity levels provides convincing monotonicity evidence.\n- Within-architecture replication (DeepSeek vs Qwen, same backbone, different training) and cross-architecture replication (LLaMA) provide good breadth.\n- Scaling from profiling (n=50) to full (n=496/475) with quantified CI narrowing demonstrates scaling discipline.\n\n**Weaknesses in coverage:**\n\n- **GPQA and 14B results are placeholder tables.** Tables 6 and 7 contain \"---\" entries with comments like \"Results pending.\" This is unacceptable for submission. These sections should either be completed or removed entirely. The paper currently promises cross-benchmark and scale analyses it cannot deliver.\n\n- **Recency control sample size:** n=100 per sparsity (vs n=496/500 for importance/random) is adequate for the *null* result claimed (power >0.85 for dz>=0.30), but the asymmetry reduces comparability. More critically, recency is tested at only s={0.15, 0.25, 0.30}, with the s=0.30 condition described as \"numerically unreliable\" due to KV perplexity saturation (>10^5). This leaves only two cleanly interpretable recency conditions.\n\n- **GSM8K sample size:** n=50 is acknowledged as underpowered for random eviction effects. The Qwen GSM8K results are \"inconclusive due to generation truncation,\" which significantly limits the cross-benchmark claim.\n\n- **No larger models actually tested.** The paper title says \"7B-Scale\" and the 14B section is a placeholder. The limitations section mentions 7B only.\n\n- **Single seed for random eviction.** The paper does not mention whether random eviction results are averaged over multiple random seeds. Given that random mask selection introduces sampling variance, a single-seed evaluation at n=496 could still carry meaningful mask-specific bias.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Reproducibility\n\n**Positive:**\n- Model names, precision (bfloat16), generation parameters (max_new_tokens=2048/8192), and seed (42 for bootstrap) are specified.\n- Phase segmentation algorithm is fully described (attention sparsity metric in Eq. 5, tercile boundaries, min_phase_length=10, merging rule).\n- Statistical methodology is exhaustively documented.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Code and data will be released upon acceptance\" is standard but not verifiable at review time.\n- The exact implementation of \"position-normalized cumulative attention score\" (Eq. 3) needs careful specification: at what point during generation is the cumulative score computed? Is it recomputed at every step? The paper says \"cumulative\" but the update frequency is not explicit.\n- Head pruning by Frobenius norm: is the norm computed once at the beginning of generation, or updated dynamically? This matters for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Statistical Validity\n\n**Well-reported:**\n- p-values with Bonferroni correction, effect sizes (Cohen's dz), bootstrap 95% CIs, and the Jonckheere-Terpstra test are all appropriately used.\n- The paper correctly reports medians rather than means given extreme skew.\n- The paired design (within-problem comparison across phases) is statistically efficient.\n\n**Issues:**\n\n- **Spearman rho=1.0 on n=4 data points.** The paper reports rho=1.0 for the dose-response monotonicity with 4 sparsity levels. With n=4, *any* monotonic sequence gives rho=1.0. The paper acknowledges \"interpret with caution\" and relies on JT tests instead, which is appropriate, but the rho=1.0 claim appears prominently in the abstract and introduction as if it carries statistical weight. It does not.\n\n- **The \"15-17x\" strategy specificity ratio.** This is computed as Delta_importance / Delta_random at s>=0.25. However, at s=0.25 on DeepSeek: 3.99/0.26 = 15.3x, and at s=0.30: 8.48/0.51 = 16.6x. These ratios are sensitive to the denominator. The random Delta at s=0.25 has CI [0.13, 0.35], meaning the ratio could range from ~11x to ~31x. The headline \"15-17x\" understates this uncertainty.\n\n- **Effect size reporting for recency.** The recency control reports dz=0.083 at s=0.15 (NS) and a significantly *negative* Delta=-0.091 at s=0.25. The negative result at s=0.25 is interesting but the paper does not discuss what it means for recency to produce a *negative* phase gradient (i.e., compound interaction is *larger* in the high-sparsity phase under recency). This deserves interpretation.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n| # | Weakness | Severity |\n|---|----------|----------|\n| W1 | Placeholder tables (GPQA Table 6, 14B Table 7) with no data. Paper claims cross-benchmark and scale validation it cannot deliver. | **Major** |\n| W2 | Spearman rho=1.0 on n=4 points used as headline evidence in abstract/intro. Misleading. | **Moderate** |\n| W3 | No control for random seed variance in random eviction. Single random mask realization per problem could introduce systematic bias. | **Moderate** |\n| W4 | Recency control has only 2 cleanly interpretable conditions (s=0.15 and s=0.25), with s=0.30 declared numerically unreliable. Insufficient for dose-response characterization of the null. | **Moderate** |\n| W5 | The \"importance discrimination collapse\" mechanism is explicitly correlational and accounts for \"less than\" the observed 15-17x divergence. The mechanistic contribution is thin. | **Moderate** |\n| W6 | GSM8K evaluation is severely underpowered (n=50) and Qwen GSM8K is \"inconclusive due to truncation.\" Cross-benchmark evidence is weaker than claimed. | **Moderate** |\n| W7 | The practical utility demonstration (pilot mitigation, 18% recovery, CI spans zero) explicitly fails its own pre-registered gate. The paper is purely descriptive with no actionable method. | **Minor** (acknowledged honestly, but weakens impact) |\n| W8 | Phase segmentation validated against `<think>` on only n=48 from a single model on GSM8K. The primary evaluation is on MATH-500 where this validation was not performed. | **Minor** |\n| W9 | The paper's title references \"7B-Scale\" but the contribution claim in the introduction references LLaMA-8B and mentions 14B. The scope is inconsistent. | **Minor** |\n| W10 | The \"15-17x\" headline ratio has wide implicit uncertainty (could be 11-31x based on denominator CIs) but is presented as a precise characterization. | **Minor** |\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Random seed control:** How many random seeds were used for random eviction experiments? If one, have you verified that the phase gradient (or lack thereof) is stable across seeds?\n\n2. **Placeholder sections:** Will GPQA and 14B results be available before submission? If not, why are these sections included?\n\n3. **Negative recency gradient at s=0.25:** Delta=-0.091 is significantly negative. What is the interpretation? Does compound sparsity under recency eviction produce *more* interaction in high-sparsity phases? This seems theoretically important but receives no discussion.\n\n4. **Computational details of importance scoring:** Is the cumulative attention score recomputed after head pruning takes effect, or computed once from the dense model? If recomputed (as implied by the \"circular dependency\" discussion), what is the update frequency?\n\n5. **Phase segmentation from dense model:** You segment phases using the dense model's attention sparsity. Under compound sparsity, do the actual attention patterns still show the same phase structure, or does compression disrupt it? If the latter, the phases you analyze may not correspond to the model's actual computational modes during compressed inference.\n\n6. **Why is LLaMA strategy specificity so much weaker (2-2.6x vs 15-17x)?** The paper attributes this to \"architectural details\" but does not provide a concrete mechanistic explanation. Given that this is a major finding (strategy specificity is not universal), it deserves deeper analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Score\n\n**Score: 4 (Borderline Reject)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a methodologically sound and statistically careful characterization of an interesting phenomenon (phase-dependent compound sparsity severity). The experimental design, particularly the three-way strategy comparison with recency as a scoring-independent control, is creative. The statistical framework is rigorous. However, several issues prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) The paper contains placeholder tables for two claimed contributions (GPQA, 14B scale), making it incomplete as submitted. (2) The contribution is purely descriptive: it characterizes an interaction but offers no method, and the pilot mitigation explicitly fails. (3) Cross-benchmark evidence beyond MATH-500 is weak (GSM8K underpowered, Qwen truncated, GPQA missing). (4) The mechanistic analysis (discrimination collapse) is explicitly correlational and quantitatively insufficient to explain the observed effects. (5) The headline statistics (rho=1.0 on n=4, \"15-17x\" without uncertainty) slightly overstate the precision of findings.\n\nThe core contribution, that compound sparsity interaction is phase-dependent and strategy-specific, is likely true and potentially useful for the efficiency community. But as submitted, the paper reads more as an extended empirical observation than a complete contribution. Removing placeholder sections, adding random seed controls, and either providing a phase-aware method that works or more deeply characterizing the mechanism would strengthen it to borderline accept."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ae7e8093c58aaec24 (use SendMessage with to: 'ae7e8093c58aaec24' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 60237\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 136669</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"470c62c8-0561-4002-91a5-c990645409f2","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:00:45.118Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity: Strategy-Specific Severity Emerges from the Interaction of Head Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (joint head pruning + KV cache eviction) produces phase-dependent interaction severity that varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Three key findings: (1) importance-based eviction has strong phase-modulated compound interaction (gradient 0.39–10.68, dz up to 1.22), (2) random masking has an onset threshold around s≈0.20–0.25 and produces 5–17× smaller effects, (3) recency-based eviction yields null phase gradients (dz<0.1), creating a three-way dissociation.\n\n**Method:**\n- Interaction Index: II_i = PPL(both_i) - PPL(prune_i) - PPL(evict_i) + PPL(base_i), measuring super-additivity\n- Phase Segmentation: tercile splits of per-token perplexity from uncompressed model → high/mid/low phases\n- Three eviction strategies: importance-based (H2O-style), random, recency-based\n- Statistical analysis: Wilcoxon signed-rank with Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs (B=10,000)\n- Models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (primary), Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B\n- Benchmarks: MATH-500 (n=500), GPQA Diamond (n=198), GSM8K\n- Sparsity: s ∈ {0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30} with matched head pruning and KV eviction\n\n**Key Tables:**\n- Table 1: GPQA importance-based II by phase. Gradient grows from 0.947(dz=0.72) at s=0.15 to 10.68(dz=1.22) at s=0.30. Monotonic high<mid<low at every sparsity.\n- Table 2: Three-way dissociation at s=0.25: Importance gradient=8.62 (dz=1.12, p=3e-34), Random=0.76 (dz=0.22, p=0.003, 11.3×), Recency≈0 (NS, >100×).\n- Table 3: Random masking dose-response: NS below s=0.20, significant at s=0.25+ but much weaker.\n- Table 4: Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH-500 (15.5–16.6×) > GPQA (7.8–11.3×) > GSM8K (2.3–3.8×).\n- Table 5: Full GPQA dose-response for both strategies.\n\n**Discussion mentions:**\n- Discrimination collapse mechanism (Gini/CV decrease)\n- LLaMA architecture: importance/random ratio only 2.0–2.6× vs 15–17× on DeepSeek\n- Pilot phase-aware allocation: 18% PPL recovery (p=0.002) but CIs cross zero\n- Limited to 7B models; recency control uses n=100 per sparsity\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe imagined experiments list includes:\n- gpqa_ds_importance at s=0.20, 0.25, 0.30 (s=0.15 presumably already run)\n- gpqa_ds_random at s=0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\n- gpqa_cross_benchmark_synthesis\n- Expected acceptance probability: 0.65, novelty=4.0, overall=3.75\n\nAll GPQA experiments are on DeepSeek with n≈198. The context mentions recency experiments but the paper says n=100 per sparsity for recency on GPQA.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions with specific, evidence-based assessments:\n\n1. **Methodological Soundness** (weight: high)\n   - Is the Interaction Index well-defined and appropriate?\n   - Is the phase segmentation methodology rigorous? (tercile splits of uncompressed PPL)\n   - Are statistical tests appropriate (Wilcoxon, Bonferroni, bootstrap)?\n   - Any circularity concerns?\n\n2. **Experimental Rigor** (weight: high)\n   - Are controls adequate? (recency as scoring-independent control)\n   - Is the coverage sufficient (3 benchmarks × 3 models × 4 sparsities × 3 strategies)?\n   - Are sample sizes adequate (n=500, n=198, n=100 for recency)?\n   - Missing experiments or ablations?\n\n3. **Reproducibility** (weight: medium)\n   - Is enough detail provided to reproduce?\n   - Are hyperparameters specified?\n   - Is code/data availability mentioned?\n\n4. **Statistical Validity** (weight: high)\n   - Are p-values, effect sizes, CIs properly reported?\n   - Multiple testing correction?\n   - Any issues with the reported numbers?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — list each weakness with severity (minor/moderate/major/critical)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — specific questions that would affect your score\n\n7. **Score** — provide a single score (1-6) with brief justification\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","agentId":"ae7e8093c58aaec24","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough reading of the paper, I now have enough information to write a comprehensive review focused on methods and experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity (R1 — Methods & Experiments)\n\n---\n\n### 1. Methodological Soundness\n\n**Interaction Index (II):**\nThe II definition (Eq. 1: II = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1) is well-motivated and standard for measuring super-additivity. The choice to normalize by dense-baseline perplexity before computing the interaction is appropriate. The verification under the multiplicative null (Appendix A) strengthens confidence that the findings are not null-choice-dependent. One concern: II is computed on per-phase *segments* of varying length, meaning II magnitudes are not directly comparable across phases unless segment-level perplexity is properly averaged. The paper uses medians of per-problem paired differences, which appropriately handles this through pairing.\n\n**Phase Segmentation:**\nThe tercile-split approach based on per-position attention sparsity from the *uncompressed* model is a reasonable operationalization. The validation against `<think>` tokens (kappa=0.88, AUC=0.98) on n=48 GSM8K problems is encouraging but narrow. Robustness to quartile/quintile splits and min_phase_length variation is well-documented.\n\n**Potential circularity concern:** The paper acknowledges that importance-based eviction uses attention scores, and phase segmentation also uses attention sparsity. However, phases are defined from the *dense* model, not the compressed model, which largely eliminates circularity. The recency control further addresses this. I find the circularity concern adequately mitigated.\n\n**Statistical framework:** Wilcoxon signed-rank (paired, nonparametric) is appropriate given the heavily right-skewed distributions (mean/median ratios up to 43:1). Bonferroni correction with k=4 per family is conservative and clearly justified. The Jonckheere-Terpstra test for dose-response monotonicity is the correct choice. Bootstrap CIs (B=10,000) are standard.\n\n**Score: Sound.** The methodology is well-specified and the choices are defensible.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Experimental Rigor\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Three eviction strategies (importance, random, recency) with recency as a scoring-independent control is an excellent experimental design choice. This is the paper's strongest methodological contribution.\n- Dose-response across 4 sparsity levels provides convincing monotonicity evidence.\n- Within-architecture replication (DeepSeek vs Qwen, same backbone, different training) and cross-architecture replication (LLaMA) provide good breadth.\n- Scaling from profiling (n=50) to full (n=496/475) with quantified CI narrowing demonstrates scaling discipline.\n\n**Weaknesses in coverage:**\n\n- **GPQA and 14B results are placeholder tables.** Tables 6 and 7 contain \"---\" entries with comments like \"Results pending.\" This is unacceptable for submission. These sections should either be completed or removed entirely. The paper currently promises cross-benchmark and scale analyses it cannot deliver.\n\n- **Recency control sample size:** n=100 per sparsity (vs n=496/500 for importance/random) is adequate for the *null* result claimed (power >0.85 for dz>=0.30), but the asymmetry reduces comparability. More critically, recency is tested at only s={0.15, 0.25, 0.30}, with the s=0.30 condition described as \"numerically unreliable\" due to KV perplexity saturation (>10^5). This leaves only two cleanly interpretable recency conditions.\n\n- **GSM8K sample size:** n=50 is acknowledged as underpowered for random eviction effects. The Qwen GSM8K results are \"inconclusive due to generation truncation,\" which significantly limits the cross-benchmark claim.\n\n- **No larger models actually tested.** The paper title says \"7B-Scale\" and the 14B section is a placeholder. The limitations section mentions 7B only.\n\n- **Single seed for random eviction.** The paper does not mention whether random eviction results are averaged over multiple random seeds. Given that random mask selection introduces sampling variance, a single-seed evaluation at n=496 could still carry meaningful mask-specific bias.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Reproducibility\n\n**Positive:**\n- Model names, precision (bfloat16), generation parameters (max_new_tokens=2048/8192), and seed (42 for bootstrap) are specified.\n- Phase segmentation algorithm is fully described (attention sparsity metric in Eq. 5, tercile boundaries, min_phase_length=10, merging rule).\n- Statistical methodology is exhaustively documented.\n\n**Concerns:**\n- \"Code and data will be released upon acceptance\" is standard but not verifiable at review time.\n- The exact implementation of \"position-normalized cumulative attention score\" (Eq. 3) needs careful specification: at what point during generation is the cumulative score computed? Is it recomputed at every step? The paper says \"cumulative\" but the update frequency is not explicit.\n- Head pruning by Frobenius norm: is the norm computed once at the beginning of generation, or updated dynamically? This matters for reproducibility.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Statistical Validity\n\n**Well-reported:**\n- p-values with Bonferroni correction, effect sizes (Cohen's dz), bootstrap 95% CIs, and the Jonckheere-Terpstra test are all appropriately used.\n- The paper correctly reports medians rather than means given extreme skew.\n- The paired design (within-problem comparison across phases) is statistically efficient.\n\n**Issues:**\n\n- **Spearman rho=1.0 on n=4 data points.** The paper reports rho=1.0 for the dose-response monotonicity with 4 sparsity levels. With n=4, *any* monotonic sequence gives rho=1.0. The paper acknowledges \"interpret with caution\" and relies on JT tests instead, which is appropriate, but the rho=1.0 claim appears prominently in the abstract and introduction as if it carries statistical weight. It does not.\n\n- **The \"15-17x\" strategy specificity ratio.** This is computed as Delta_importance / Delta_random at s>=0.25. However, at s=0.25 on DeepSeek: 3.99/0.26 = 15.3x, and at s=0.30: 8.48/0.51 = 16.6x. These ratios are sensitive to the denominator. The random Delta at s=0.25 has CI [0.13, 0.35], meaning the ratio could range from ~11x to ~31x. The headline \"15-17x\" understates this uncertainty.\n\n- **Effect size reporting for recency.** The recency control reports dz=0.083 at s=0.15 (NS) and a significantly *negative* Delta=-0.091 at s=0.25. The negative result at s=0.25 is interesting but the paper does not discuss what it means for recency to produce a *negative* phase gradient (i.e., compound interaction is *larger* in the high-sparsity phase under recency). This deserves interpretation.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n| # | Weakness | Severity |\n|---|----------|----------|\n| W1 | Placeholder tables (GPQA Table 6, 14B Table 7) with no data. Paper claims cross-benchmark and scale validation it cannot deliver. | **Major** |\n| W2 | Spearman rho=1.0 on n=4 points used as headline evidence in abstract/intro. Misleading. | **Moderate** |\n| W3 | No control for random seed variance in random eviction. Single random mask realization per problem could introduce systematic bias. | **Moderate** |\n| W4 | Recency control has only 2 cleanly interpretable conditions (s=0.15 and s=0.25), with s=0.30 declared numerically unreliable. Insufficient for dose-response characterization of the null. | **Moderate** |\n| W5 | The \"importance discrimination collapse\" mechanism is explicitly correlational and accounts for \"less than\" the observed 15-17x divergence. The mechanistic contribution is thin. | **Moderate** |\n| W6 | GSM8K evaluation is severely underpowered (n=50) and Qwen GSM8K is \"inconclusive due to truncation.\" Cross-benchmark evidence is weaker than claimed. | **Moderate** |\n| W7 | The practical utility demonstration (pilot mitigation, 18% recovery, CI spans zero) explicitly fails its own pre-registered gate. The paper is purely descriptive with no actionable method. | **Minor** (acknowledged honestly, but weakens impact) |\n| W8 | Phase segmentation validated against `<think>` on only n=48 from a single model on GSM8K. The primary evaluation is on MATH-500 where this validation was not performed. | **Minor** |\n| W9 | The paper's title references \"7B-Scale\" but the contribution claim in the introduction references LLaMA-8B and mentions 14B. The scope is inconsistent. | **Minor** |\n| W10 | The \"15-17x\" headline ratio has wide implicit uncertainty (could be 11-31x based on denominator CIs) but is presented as a precise characterization. | **Minor** |\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Random seed control:** How many random seeds were used for random eviction experiments? If one, have you verified that the phase gradient (or lack thereof) is stable across seeds?\n\n2. **Placeholder sections:** Will GPQA and 14B results be available before submission? If not, why are these sections included?\n\n3. **Negative recency gradient at s=0.25:** Delta=-0.091 is significantly negative. What is the interpretation? Does compound sparsity under recency eviction produce *more* interaction in high-sparsity phases? This seems theoretically important but receives no discussion.\n\n4. **Computational details of importance scoring:** Is the cumulative attention score recomputed after head pruning takes effect, or computed once from the dense model? If recomputed (as implied by the \"circular dependency\" discussion), what is the update frequency?\n\n5. **Phase segmentation from dense model:** You segment phases using the dense model's attention sparsity. Under compound sparsity, do the actual attention patterns still show the same phase structure, or does compression disrupt it? If the latter, the phases you analyze may not correspond to the model's actual computational modes during compressed inference.\n\n6. **Why is LLaMA strategy specificity so much weaker (2-2.6x vs 15-17x)?** The paper attributes this to \"architectural details\" but does not provide a concrete mechanistic explanation. Given that this is a major finding (strategy specificity is not universal), it deserves deeper analysis.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Score\n\n**Score: 4 (Borderline Reject)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper presents a methodologically sound and statistically careful characterization of an interesting phenomenon (phase-dependent compound sparsity severity). The experimental design, particularly the three-way strategy comparison with recency as a scoring-independent control, is creative. The statistical framework is rigorous. However, several issues prevent acceptance:\n\n(1) The paper contains placeholder tables for two claimed contributions (GPQA, 14B scale), making it incomplete as submitted. (2) The contribution is purely descriptive: it characterizes an interaction but offers no method, and the pilot mitigation explicitly fails. (3) Cross-benchmark evidence beyond MATH-500 is weak (GSM8K underpowered, Qwen truncated, GPQA missing). (4) The mechanistic analysis (discrimination collapse) is explicitly correlational and quantitatively insufficient to explain the observed effects. (5) The headline statistics (rho=1.0 on n=4, \"15-17x\" without uncertainty) slightly overstate the precision of findings.\n\nThe core contribution, that compound sparsity interaction is phase-dependent and strategy-specific, is likely true and potentially useful for the efficiency community. But as submitted, the paper reads more as an extended empirical observation than a complete contribution. Removing placeholder sections, adding random seed controls, and either providing a phase-aware method that works or more deeply characterizing the mechanism would strengthen it to borderline accept."}],"totalDurationMs":136669,"totalTokens":60237,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3271,"cache_read_input_tokens":54016,"output_tokens":2949,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3271},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2949,"cache_read_input_tokens":54016,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3271,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3271,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"aceb691885f65aaf3","tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":55875,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":118630},"uuid":"d6eac684-7873-4e37-b060-e2ead935a44f","session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BUXcq33eMmeRfm7tsABxTn","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me compile my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThis paper investigates the interaction between attention head pruning and KV cache eviction (\"compound sparsity\") and reports that the severity of super-additive degradation varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. The main finding is that importance-based eviction produces a phase-dependent severity gradient (amplified in low-sparsity phases) while random and recency-based eviction do not (or produce much weaker gradients). The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper targeting EMNLP 2026.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely new?**\n\nPartially. The individual components are not new: (a) compound compression produces super-additive degradation (SparK, SLiM), (b) compression sensitivity is phase-dependent (ThinKV, decision representation transitions paper), (c) importance score collapse exists under pruning (HIES). The novelty lies in the specific combination: showing that the interaction severity between head pruning and KV eviction is jointly modulated by phase and strategy, and quantifying the dose-response. This is a reasonable incremental contribution but not a surprising finding once you combine existing knowledge. If head pruning degrades importance scores (HIES), and importance-based eviction relies on those scores, then of course the compound interaction will be strongest where attention is most distributed (low-sparsity phases) and where importance scoring is most critical. The logical chain from prior work to this finding is short.\n\n**Is the three-way dissociation experimental design novel?**\n\nThe use of recency eviction as a methodological control (rather than just a performance baseline) is a genuinely clever experimental design choice. The paper claims this has no precedent in the KV cache literature, which appears accurate. However, the novelty of an experimental control is a relatively modest contribution on its own.\n\n**Is discrimination collapse a new mechanism or repackaging?**\n\nThe paper honestly acknowledges that HIES already reported importance score collapse under single-axis pruning. The extension to the compound setting, while useful, is incremental. The paper correctly notes it is correlational and cannot explain the full 15-17x divergence, which weakens its explanatory power.\n\n**How does this advance beyond existing work?**\n\nThe advance is primarily quantitative characterization of a phenomenon that is qualitatively predictable from prior work. The paper provides careful statistics and multi-model replication, which is valuable but does not constitute a conceptual breakthrough.\n\n**Verdict:** The novelty is below the threshold for a top venue. The finding is a natural combination of known phenomena (compound interaction + phase sensitivity + score collapse) rather than a genuinely surprising discovery.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Reference count:** The actual paper contains substantially more than 9 references. The Related Work section cites approximately 30+ papers across structured pruning, KV cache eviction, compound compression, phase-aware inference, and conditional computation. The literature map contains 30+ entries. This is adequate for EMNLP.\n\n**Coverage quality:** The Related Work is well-organized and comprehensive for the core areas. Key papers (SparK, ThinKV, HIES, SLiM, StreamingLLM, H2O, GRASPrune, DefensiveKV) are cited and positioned appropriately.\n\n**Potential gaps:**\n- Multi-axis compression ordering (covered via Kim et al. 2026)\n- Adaptive KV cache methods (covered: Ada-KV, Twilight, LAVa, LKV)\n- The structured pruning paragraph is somewhat thin (only classical references: Voita, Michel, magnitude pruning, lottery ticket, movement pruning). More recent LLM-specific structured pruning work (e.g., ShortGPT, SliceGPT, LaCo) could strengthen this.\n- The connection to MoE and conditional computation is acknowledged but underdeveloped.\n\n**Positioning accuracy:** The paper's positioning against prior work is generally honest. It correctly identifies that SparK treats interaction as phase-invariant and ThinKV considers only single-axis compression. The framing document's \"MUST NOT\" constraints (e.g., not framing this as \"importance is broken\") show deliberate care.\n\n**Verdict:** Related work coverage is adequate. No critical omissions that would change the paper's positioning.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact\n\n**Does this change how practitioners think about compound compression?**\n\nModestly. The practical takeaway (\"be more careful in low-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction\") is useful but not transformative. The paper provides no deployable method, and the pilot mitigation experiment failed its pre-registered gate (18% recovery, CI crosses zero).\n\n**Is the lack of method contribution a problem?**\n\nFor EMNLP, empirical finding papers are acceptable, but the finding must be surprising or broadly impactful. The finding here is interesting but largely predictable: if you degrade the scoring mechanism (via head pruning) and then rely on that degraded scoring for eviction, the interaction will be worst where scoring is most critical. The direction of the finding is obvious; its magnitude (15-17x) is the interesting part.\n\n**Would this be cited and built upon?**\n\nYes, by researchers building phase-aware compression systems. The framework (Interaction Index decomposed by phase) could become a standard evaluation tool. The practical impact is limited to a niche community, however.\n\n**Verdict:** Moderate significance. Useful quantitative characterization but limited practical impact without a working method.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Extremely thorough statistical methodology (Wilcoxon, Bonferroni, bootstrap CIs, Jonckheere-Terpstra, effect sizes)\n- Honest limitations section acknowledging correlational nature, failed mitigation pilot, model-dependent effects\n- Clear tables with proper CIs\n- Multiple replication attempts (cross-model, cross-architecture, cross-benchmark)\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- The paper is dense and repetitive. The same numbers (0.39 to 8.48, 15-17x, etc.) appear in the Abstract, Introduction, Results, Discussion, and Conclusion.\n- Placeholder tables (Tables 5, 6 for GPQA and 14B experiments) remain in the manuscript with \"Results pending.\" This is clearly a draft that is not submission-ready.\n- The Interaction Index formula is defined as the additive deviation from independence, but labeled \"II\" without explaining why the multiplicative null is secondary. The justification is buried in a parenthetical.\n- The title is descriptive but does not communicate the key finding concisely.\n- Violations of stated style guide: the Abstract uses dashes (em-dashes/en-dashes) despite the paper-writing-norms explicitly prohibiting them.\n\n**Overclaiming:**\n- Calling recency eviction a \"methodological contribution\" and \"novel experimental design\" is slight overclaiming. Using a simpler baseline as a control is good experimental practice but not a contribution per se.\n- \"Phase-gated analysis framework\" framing makes a simple decomposition sound more significant than it is. Computing II separately per phase is not really a \"framework.\"\n- Claiming the finding \"challenges the implicit assumption of uniform compound degradation\" overstates how strongly prior work assumed uniformity versus simply not measuring phase dependence.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n| # | Weakness | Severity |\n|---|----------|----------|\n| W1 | Core finding is predictable from combining prior work (HIES + SparK + ThinKV). The logical chain \"head pruning degrades importance scores, so importance-based eviction suffers more in phases where scoring matters most\" is short. | **Major** |\n| W2 | No deployable method. The pilot mitigation failed its gate (18% recovery, CI crosses zero). The paper provides a diagnostic but no solution. | **Major** |\n| W3 | Incomplete manuscript: placeholder tables for GPQA Diamond and Qwen2.5-14B experiments with \"Results pending.\" The paper as submitted cannot be reviewed as a complete contribution. | **Major** |\n| W4 | All models are 7-8B scale. Strategy specificity already attenuates from 15-17x to 2.0-2.6x going from DeepSeek to LLaMA, and from 15-17x to 2.3-3.8x going from MATH-500 to GSM8K. The finding may be specific to particular model/task combinations rather than general. | **Moderate** |\n| W5 | The \"discrimination collapse\" mechanism is correlational and cannot explain the 15-17x divergence (both strategies show identical Gini drops). This is acknowledged but undermines the mechanistic contribution. | **Moderate** |\n| W6 | Recency control uses only n=100 (vs. n=500 for main experiments) and only on DeepSeek. The s=0.30 condition is numerically unreliable. This is the paper's main methodological control, but it has limited statistical power relative to the main experiments. | **Moderate** |\n| W7 | The crossover phenomenon (C3) is model-dependent (present in DeepSeek, absent in Qwen), limiting its generalizability as a finding. | **Minor** |\n| W8 | GSM8K evaluation uses only n=50, and Qwen GSM8K results are \"inconclusive due to generation truncation.\" | **Minor** |\n| W9 | Phase segmentation validated against <think> tokens only on one model (DeepSeek) on one benchmark (GSM8K, n=48). | **Minor** |\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Predictability of finding:** Given that HIES already shows importance score collapse under pruning, and ThinKV shows phase-dependent compression tolerance, what specific aspect of your finding would NOT be predicted by simply combining these two prior results? What is the \"surprise\" beyond quantification?\n\n2. **Attenuation pattern:** Strategy specificity drops from 15-17x (DeepSeek/MATH-500) to 2.0-2.6x (LLaMA) to 2.3-3.8x (GSM8K). At what point does the effect become practically negligible? Is the phenomenon robust or is it concentrated in one favorable configuration?\n\n3. **Incomplete experiments:** What are the GPQA Diamond and Qwen2.5-14B results? These are listed in Section 5.5 and 5.4 respectively with placeholder tables. Can you provide these results?\n\n4. **Alternative explanation:** Could the phase gradient simply reflect that low-sparsity phases have higher baseline perplexity (you note dense PPL is 1.18 for low vs. 1.10 for high), meaning the same absolute perturbation manifests as a larger PPL ratio? Have you controlled for baseline difficulty?\n\n5. **Practical implication:** Given the mitigation pilot failed (18% recovery, CI crosses zero), what concrete engineering action should a practitioner take based on your findings beyond \"be aware this interaction exists\"?\n\n---\n\n### 7. Score\n\n**Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper is a careful empirical study with thorough statistical methodology and honest limitations. However, it falls below the acceptance bar for several reasons: (1) the core finding is largely predictable from combining existing knowledge about importance score collapse and phase-dependent compression sensitivity; (2) it provides no deployable method, and the mitigation pilot failed; (3) the manuscript is incomplete (placeholder tables for two experiments); (4) the phenomenon attenuates substantially across architectures and tasks, raising questions about generality; (5) the mechanistic contribution (discrimination collapse) is acknowledged as correlational and insufficient to explain the observed divergence. The paper would be strengthened by either demonstrating the finding at larger scale with a working phase-aware method, or by identifying a genuinely surprising aspect of the interaction that cannot be predicted from prior work. For EMNLP, the contribution is below the 25% acceptance threshold.\n\n**Confidence:** 4/5 (I have read the full paper carefully and am familiar with the KV cache compression literature, but I acknowledge the possibility that the quantitative surprise value of the 15-17x ratio may be underappreciated by this review)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aceb691885f65aaf3 (use SendMessage with to: 'aceb691885f65aaf3' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 57938\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 118631</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7283b627-1227-4165-befd-d2d2cc7a2bc4","uuid":"de9e02b7-dbaf-4ac3-9a4b-45889922b955","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:00:49.827Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **novelty and related work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity: Strategy-Specific Severity Emerges from the Interaction of Head Pruning and KV Cache Eviction\n\n**Core claim:** Compound sparsity (joint head pruning + KV cache eviction) produces phase-dependent interaction severity that varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper, not a method contribution.\n\n**Five stated contributions:**\n1. Discovery of phase-dependent compound sparsity severity as emergent property, replicated across 3 benchmarks and 3 architectures\n2. Quantified dose-response relationship with sub-exponential growth\n3. Three-way dissociation (importance sig, random weak/null, recency null) as novel experimental design for ruling out confounds\n4. Discrimination collapse as primary mechanism (Gini/CV decrease under head pruning)\n5. Cross-benchmark analysis: strategy specificity correlates with reasoning chain structure, not task difficulty\n\n**Related Work (Section 2):**\n- Compound Compression: SparK [1] (pruning+quantization, uniform super-additive), SLiM [2] (joint pruning-eviction, no phase analysis)\n- Phase-Aware KV Cache: ThinKV [3] (phase-aware single-axis), R-KV [4] (reasoning-optimized, single-axis)\n- Compression Phase Transitions: [5] (physics of KV cache compression, orthogonal \"phase\" meaning)\n- Importance Score Collapse: HIES [6] (entropy-based compensation, single-axis)\n- Adaptive Eviction: Lethe [7], H2O [8], StreamingLLM [9]\n\nTotal references: 9\n\n**Method details:**\n- Interaction Index: II_i = PPL(both) - PPL(prune) - PPL(evict) + PPL(base)\n- Phase segmentation via tercile splits of uncompressed per-token PPL\n- Three eviction strategies: importance (H2O), random, recency\n- Paired Wilcoxon with Bonferroni, Cohen's dz, bootstrap CIs\n- Models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (all 7-8B)\n- Benchmarks: MATH-500, GPQA Diamond, GSM8K\n\n**Key results:**\n- Importance gradient on GPQA: 0.947 (s=0.15) to 10.68 (s=0.30), dz=0.72–1.22\n- Three-way dissociation at s=0.25: importance 8.62 vs random 0.76 vs recency ≈0\n- Cross-benchmark specificity: MATH-500 (15–17×) > GPQA (7.8–11.3×) > GSM8K (2.3–3.8×)\n- LLaMA ratio only 2.0–2.6× (architecture-dependent)\n- Pilot phase-aware allocation: 18% PPL recovery but CIs cross zero\n\n**Limitations acknowledged:** 7B models only, GSM8K ratios modest, recency n=100, no wall-clock throughput, empirical understanding not deployable method.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the following dimensions with specific, evidence-based assessments:\n\n1. **Novelty Assessment** (weight: highest)\n   - Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely new?\n   - Is the three-way dissociation experimental design novel?\n   - Is discrimination collapse a new mechanism or repackaging of known phenomena?\n   - How does this advance the field beyond existing work on compound compression?\n   - Is this an empirical finding paper with sufficient novelty for a top venue?\n\n2. **Related Work Coverage** (weight: high)\n   - 9 references total — is this sufficient for a top venue?\n   - Are there major missing related works? Consider:\n     * KV cache compression methods beyond H2O/StreamingLLM\n     * Structured pruning literature\n     * Compound/joint compression beyond SparK/SLiM\n     * Phase/stage analysis in reasoning\n     * Attention score analysis literature\n   - Is the positioning against prior work accurate?\n   - Does the paper honestly characterize its incremental contribution vs prior work?\n\n3. **Significance and Impact** (weight: high)\n   - Does this finding change how practitioners think about compound compression?\n   - Is the lack of a method contribution a problem?\n   - Would this be cited and built upon?\n\n4. **Presentation Quality** (weight: medium)\n   - Is the paper well-written and clearly structured?\n   - Are claims well-supported by evidence?\n   - Any overclaiming?\n\n5. **Weaknesses** — list each weakness with severity (minor/moderate/major/critical)\n\n6. **Questions for Authors** — specific questions that would affect your score\n\n7. **Score** — provide a single score (1-6) with brief justification\n\nFormat your response as a structured review. Be rigorous but fair.","agentId":"aceb691885f65aaf3","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me compile my review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Phase-Dependent Compound Sparsity\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nThis paper investigates the interaction between attention head pruning and KV cache eviction (\"compound sparsity\") and reports that the severity of super-additive degradation varies by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. The main finding is that importance-based eviction produces a phase-dependent severity gradient (amplified in low-sparsity phases) while random and recency-based eviction do not (or produce much weaker gradients). The paper is positioned as an empirical finding paper targeting EMNLP 2026.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Novelty Assessment\n\n**Is the core finding (phase-dependent compound severity) genuinely new?**\n\nPartially. The individual components are not new: (a) compound compression produces super-additive degradation (SparK, SLiM), (b) compression sensitivity is phase-dependent (ThinKV, decision representation transitions paper), (c) importance score collapse exists under pruning (HIES). The novelty lies in the specific combination: showing that the interaction severity between head pruning and KV eviction is jointly modulated by phase and strategy, and quantifying the dose-response. This is a reasonable incremental contribution but not a surprising finding once you combine existing knowledge. If head pruning degrades importance scores (HIES), and importance-based eviction relies on those scores, then of course the compound interaction will be strongest where attention is most distributed (low-sparsity phases) and where importance scoring is most critical. The logical chain from prior work to this finding is short.\n\n**Is the three-way dissociation experimental design novel?**\n\nThe use of recency eviction as a methodological control (rather than just a performance baseline) is a genuinely clever experimental design choice. The paper claims this has no precedent in the KV cache literature, which appears accurate. However, the novelty of an experimental control is a relatively modest contribution on its own.\n\n**Is discrimination collapse a new mechanism or repackaging?**\n\nThe paper honestly acknowledges that HIES already reported importance score collapse under single-axis pruning. The extension to the compound setting, while useful, is incremental. The paper correctly notes it is correlational and cannot explain the full 15-17x divergence, which weakens its explanatory power.\n\n**How does this advance beyond existing work?**\n\nThe advance is primarily quantitative characterization of a phenomenon that is qualitatively predictable from prior work. The paper provides careful statistics and multi-model replication, which is valuable but does not constitute a conceptual breakthrough.\n\n**Verdict:** The novelty is below the threshold for a top venue. The finding is a natural combination of known phenomena (compound interaction + phase sensitivity + score collapse) rather than a genuinely surprising discovery.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Related Work Coverage\n\n**Reference count:** The actual paper contains substantially more than 9 references. The Related Work section cites approximately 30+ papers across structured pruning, KV cache eviction, compound compression, phase-aware inference, and conditional computation. The literature map contains 30+ entries. This is adequate for EMNLP.\n\n**Coverage quality:** The Related Work is well-organized and comprehensive for the core areas. Key papers (SparK, ThinKV, HIES, SLiM, StreamingLLM, H2O, GRASPrune, DefensiveKV) are cited and positioned appropriately.\n\n**Potential gaps:**\n- Multi-axis compression ordering (covered via Kim et al. 2026)\n- Adaptive KV cache methods (covered: Ada-KV, Twilight, LAVa, LKV)\n- The structured pruning paragraph is somewhat thin (only classical references: Voita, Michel, magnitude pruning, lottery ticket, movement pruning). More recent LLM-specific structured pruning work (e.g., ShortGPT, SliceGPT, LaCo) could strengthen this.\n- The connection to MoE and conditional computation is acknowledged but underdeveloped.\n\n**Positioning accuracy:** The paper's positioning against prior work is generally honest. It correctly identifies that SparK treats interaction as phase-invariant and ThinKV considers only single-axis compression. The framing document's \"MUST NOT\" constraints (e.g., not framing this as \"importance is broken\") show deliberate care.\n\n**Verdict:** Related work coverage is adequate. No critical omissions that would change the paper's positioning.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Significance and Impact\n\n**Does this change how practitioners think about compound compression?**\n\nModestly. The practical takeaway (\"be more careful in low-sparsity phases under importance-based eviction\") is useful but not transformative. The paper provides no deployable method, and the pilot mitigation experiment failed its pre-registered gate (18% recovery, CI crosses zero).\n\n**Is the lack of method contribution a problem?**\n\nFor EMNLP, empirical finding papers are acceptable, but the finding must be surprising or broadly impactful. The finding here is interesting but largely predictable: if you degrade the scoring mechanism (via head pruning) and then rely on that degraded scoring for eviction, the interaction will be worst where scoring is most critical. The direction of the finding is obvious; its magnitude (15-17x) is the interesting part.\n\n**Would this be cited and built upon?**\n\nYes, by researchers building phase-aware compression systems. The framework (Interaction Index decomposed by phase) could become a standard evaluation tool. The practical impact is limited to a niche community, however.\n\n**Verdict:** Moderate significance. Useful quantitative characterization but limited practical impact without a working method.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Presentation Quality\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Extremely thorough statistical methodology (Wilcoxon, Bonferroni, bootstrap CIs, Jonckheere-Terpstra, effect sizes)\n- Honest limitations section acknowledging correlational nature, failed mitigation pilot, model-dependent effects\n- Clear tables with proper CIs\n- Multiple replication attempts (cross-model, cross-architecture, cross-benchmark)\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n- The paper is dense and repetitive. The same numbers (0.39 to 8.48, 15-17x, etc.) appear in the Abstract, Introduction, Results, Discussion, and Conclusion.\n- Placeholder tables (Tables 5, 6 for GPQA and 14B experiments) remain in the manuscript with \"Results pending.\" This is clearly a draft that is not submission-ready.\n- The Interaction Index formula is defined as the additive deviation from independence, but labeled \"II\" without explaining why the multiplicative null is secondary. The justification is buried in a parenthetical.\n- The title is descriptive but does not communicate the key finding concisely.\n- Violations of stated style guide: the Abstract uses dashes (em-dashes/en-dashes) despite the paper-writing-norms explicitly prohibiting them.\n\n**Overclaiming:**\n- Calling recency eviction a \"methodological contribution\" and \"novel experimental design\" is slight overclaiming. Using a simpler baseline as a control is good experimental practice but not a contribution per se.\n- \"Phase-gated analysis framework\" framing makes a simple decomposition sound more significant than it is. Computing II separately per phase is not really a \"framework.\"\n- Claiming the finding \"challenges the implicit assumption of uniform compound degradation\" overstates how strongly prior work assumed uniformity versus simply not measuring phase dependence.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Weaknesses\n\n| # | Weakness | Severity |\n|---|----------|----------|\n| W1 | Core finding is predictable from combining prior work (HIES + SparK + ThinKV). The logical chain \"head pruning degrades importance scores, so importance-based eviction suffers more in phases where scoring matters most\" is short. | **Major** |\n| W2 | No deployable method. The pilot mitigation failed its gate (18% recovery, CI crosses zero). The paper provides a diagnostic but no solution. | **Major** |\n| W3 | Incomplete manuscript: placeholder tables for GPQA Diamond and Qwen2.5-14B experiments with \"Results pending.\" The paper as submitted cannot be reviewed as a complete contribution. | **Major** |\n| W4 | All models are 7-8B scale. Strategy specificity already attenuates from 15-17x to 2.0-2.6x going from DeepSeek to LLaMA, and from 15-17x to 2.3-3.8x going from MATH-500 to GSM8K. The finding may be specific to particular model/task combinations rather than general. | **Moderate** |\n| W5 | The \"discrimination collapse\" mechanism is correlational and cannot explain the 15-17x divergence (both strategies show identical Gini drops). This is acknowledged but undermines the mechanistic contribution. | **Moderate** |\n| W6 | Recency control uses only n=100 (vs. n=500 for main experiments) and only on DeepSeek. The s=0.30 condition is numerically unreliable. This is the paper's main methodological control, but it has limited statistical power relative to the main experiments. | **Moderate** |\n| W7 | The crossover phenomenon (C3) is model-dependent (present in DeepSeek, absent in Qwen), limiting its generalizability as a finding. | **Minor** |\n| W8 | GSM8K evaluation uses only n=50, and Qwen GSM8K results are \"inconclusive due to generation truncation.\" | **Minor** |\n| W9 | Phase segmentation validated against <think> tokens only on one model (DeepSeek) on one benchmark (GSM8K, n=48). | **Minor** |\n\n---\n\n### 6. Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Predictability of finding:** Given that HIES already shows importance score collapse under pruning, and ThinKV shows phase-dependent compression tolerance, what specific aspect of your finding would NOT be predicted by simply combining these two prior results? What is the \"surprise\" beyond quantification?\n\n2. **Attenuation pattern:** Strategy specificity drops from 15-17x (DeepSeek/MATH-500) to 2.0-2.6x (LLaMA) to 2.3-3.8x (GSM8K). At what point does the effect become practically negligible? Is the phenomenon robust or is it concentrated in one favorable configuration?\n\n3. **Incomplete experiments:** What are the GPQA Diamond and Qwen2.5-14B results? These are listed in Section 5.5 and 5.4 respectively with placeholder tables. Can you provide these results?\n\n4. **Alternative explanation:** Could the phase gradient simply reflect that low-sparsity phases have higher baseline perplexity (you note dense PPL is 1.18 for low vs. 1.10 for high), meaning the same absolute perturbation manifests as a larger PPL ratio? Have you controlled for baseline difficulty?\n\n5. **Practical implication:** Given the mitigation pilot failed (18% recovery, CI crosses zero), what concrete engineering action should a practitioner take based on your findings beyond \"be aware this interaction exists\"?\n\n---\n\n### 7. Score\n\n**Score: 3 (Borderline Reject)**\n\n**Justification:** The paper is a careful empirical study with thorough statistical methodology and honest limitations. However, it falls below the acceptance bar for several reasons: (1) the core finding is largely predictable from combining existing knowledge about importance score collapse and phase-dependent compression sensitivity; (2) it provides no deployable method, and the mitigation pilot failed; (3) the manuscript is incomplete (placeholder tables for two experiments); (4) the phenomenon attenuates substantially across architectures and tasks, raising questions about generality; (5) the mechanistic contribution (discrimination collapse) is acknowledged as correlational and insufficient to explain the observed divergence. The paper would be strengthened by either demonstrating the finding at larger scale with a working phase-aware method, or by identifying a genuinely surprising aspect of the interaction that cannot be predicted from prior work. For EMNLP, the contribution is below the 25% acceptance threshold.\n\n**Confidence:** 4/5 (I have read the full paper carefully and am familiar with the KV cache compression literature, but I acknowledge the possibility that the quantitative surprise value of the 15-17x ratio may be underappreciated by this review)."}],"totalDurationMs":118631,"totalTokens":57938,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1613,"cache_read_input_tokens":53450,"output_tokens":2874,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1613},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2874,"cache_read_input_tokens":53450,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1613,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1613,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":1,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
